A: You should use the PexObserve instead of Debug.Assert to observe the state of the objects in your test.
For example, you can use:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

to view the value of New_arrListLastIndexOfX after the operation. You should do similar actions for other variables you want to monitor.
It's important to note that the PexObserve data is only used in the generated UI of PexRunner, not in your unit test assertions. The assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" is not valid for Pex because it doesn't have enough information to execute effectively.

A: I want to clarify that, for Pex to be helpful and provide accurate results, it's important for the test to be deterministic in nature. If your tests might not return the same results (randomness included), it could influence your testing results and not give you accurate information about the software quality.
Also, you're testing methods that are mutating the state of an object, which is perfectly fine as long as you're testing for edge cases or exceptions. Pex ensures you're testing all combinations of potential inputs. 
In your case, you're probably testing adding the same element multiple times, which is fine. The problem is that Pex might generate the same index for several tests when they're on the same element and count of the list doesn't increase.
In order to avoid Pex duplicating a method with the same inputs, you might want to test only edge cases or cases that increase the list count. For example, first let Pex add 1 element, then let Pex re-add that same element under different indices or to a different list, and so on. 
Another potential problem could be with your assumptions in your tests. Ensure the assume condition you've set is true or false for the given set of inputs. Also, if you have other assumptions being made about the object or environment in which the method is being tested, they should also hold true during the run of the test. 
Remember also that Pex tests are based on testing for correctness and not performance optimization. So you might want to focus on the test cases that result in more than 50% code coverage. 
To sum up, the problem could be not so much with Pex itself but with how you use tests and assumptions.

A: I understand your needs for a solution, however there are a couple of considerations and limitations I have to remind you about:


*

*Currently Pex does not provide a built in mechanism to compare objects or collections as it stands on 2010-11-13. You will need to manually verify the object state and assert your own conditions.

*Pex generates tests in an order to ensure that all potential valid inputs are tested but does not guarantee that all edge cases are considered. You should consider designing your tests in such a way that they test all potential edge cases and edge conditions. 

*Assertions should not be used as part of test design - asserts only serve to signal errors during a debug process, therefore they may interfere with the testing that Pex itself is designed to do.

*Testing of contract and exception conditions is a key aspect of static code analysis (and automated testing typically covers only a small part of such static analysis). Some contracts might not be able to be tested automatically due to reasons like absence of a 'contract' for a method (e.g. a constructor or destructor).


In your tests, I must recommend to assert values like the Count or the returned value from methods and verify the added items have been correctly inserted. You could also make use of object snapshots to verify the state of the list after each operation. 
But remember to let Pex play its part - test for its coverage and effectiveness. 
Doing manual checks post Pex test runs will help to--------------------	    
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@ if value was inserted at position greater than count it returns "-1"
	
	    }
    }

}

